1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,082
The poster was finished on Tuesday. By Wednesday, June

2
00:00:04,082 --> 00:00:08,164
had decided the moon was too large. By Thursday,

3
00:00:08,164 --> 00:00:12,246
she had decided the entire sky was wrong.

4
00:00:12,666 --> 00:00:17,452
Twenty-three versions lay across her studio floor. Each solved

5
00:00:17,452 --> 00:00:22,238
something in the previous one. Each seemed to reveal

6
00:00:22,238 --> 00:00:27,024
a new mistake she had somehow failed to see.

7
00:00:27,444 --> 00:00:30,497
The poster was for a small neighborhood exhibition. Nothing

8
00:00:30,497 --> 00:00:33,550
prestigious. A friend had asked her to bring one

9
00:00:33,550 --> 00:00:36,604
print. The deadline was Saturday, and she had said

10
00:00:36,604 --> 00:00:39,657
yes.

11
00:00:40,077 --> 00:00:43,872
In her head, however, the room had become enormous.

12
00:00:43,872 --> 00:00:47,667
Every visitor would stop at her corner. Every weakness

13
00:00:47,667 --> 00:00:51,462
in the drawing would say something permanent about her.

14
00:00:51,882 --> 00:00:55,008
June was not avoiding the work. She worked constantly.

15
00:00:55,008 --> 00:00:58,133
She sharpened pencils to needles, mixed another color, and

16
00:00:58,133 --> 00:01:01,258
scraped yesterday's certainty from the edge of the printing

17
00:01:01,258 --> 00:01:04,383
block.

18
00:01:04,803 --> 00:01:09,116
At midnight, the lamp still burned. She changed the

19
00:01:09,116 --> 00:01:13,429
spacing between two letters, stepped back, and waited for

20
00:01:13,429 --> 00:01:17,741
the feeling that would tell her she could stop.

21
00:01:18,161 --> 00:01:22,023
The feeling never came. A clean edge made a

22
00:01:22,023 --> 00:01:25,885
rough one noticeable. A better shadow made the lettering

23
00:01:25,885 --> 00:01:29,747
seem weak. Improvement kept moving the finish line.

24
00:01:30,167 --> 00:01:32,999
On Friday, she carried a test print to the

25
00:01:32,999 --> 00:01:35,831
frame shop. Rain freckled the protective sleeve. She held

26
00:01:35,831 --> 00:01:38,663
it against her chest as if it could hear

27
00:01:38,663 --> 00:01:41,495
her doubts.

28
00:01:41,915 --> 00:01:44,862
The framer asked whether it was the final version.

29
00:01:44,862 --> 00:01:47,809
June opened her mouth and realized she had no

30
00:01:47,809 --> 00:01:50,757
clear definition of final, only a list of reasons

31
00:01:50,757 --> 00:01:53,704
it was not.

32
00:01:54,124 --> 00:01:57,360
He handed her a scrap of card to record

33
00:01:57,360 --> 00:02:00,596
the dimensions. She wrote the exhibition title instead. Then

34
00:02:00,596 --> 00:02:03,832
the paper size. Those were facts she could actually

35
00:02:03,832 --> 00:02:07,068
answer.

36
00:02:07,488 --> 00:02:10,718
Back home, she separated the brief from the moving

37
00:02:10,718 --> 00:02:13,947
target. The brief asked for a readable title, a

38
00:02:13,947 --> 00:02:17,177
clear scene, and the agreed size. Her fear asked

39
00:02:17,177 --> 00:02:20,406
for universal approval.

40
00:02:20,826 --> 00:02:24,114
Universal approval had not been part of the commission.

41
00:02:24,114 --> 00:02:27,402
It had slipped in quietly and taken control of

42
00:02:27,402 --> 00:02:30,689
every decision. No amount of careful printing could guarantee

43
00:02:30,689 --> 00:02:33,977
it.

44
00:02:34,397 --> 00:02:37,661
She opened an old sketchbook. The drawings were clumsy

45
00:02:37,661 --> 00:02:40,925
in places, but she remembered making them. A crooked

46
00:02:40,925 --> 00:02:44,190
roof carried more life than some of her polished

47
00:02:44,190 --> 00:02:47,454
recent work.

48
00:02:47,874 --> 00:02:51,115
This did not mean mistakes were secretly better. It

49
00:02:51,115 --> 00:02:54,357
meant a picture could have something worth seeing before

50
00:02:54,357 --> 00:02:57,598
every problem in it had disappeared. She had forgotten

51
00:02:57,598 --> 00:03:00,839
that distinction.

52
00:03:01,259 --> 00:03:03,943
June set a test print beneath the lamp. She

53
00:03:03,943 --> 00:03:06,628
read the title from the doorway. She checked the

54
00:03:06,628 --> 00:03:09,312
paper size. She looked for the shape that carried

55
00:03:09,312 --> 00:03:11,997
the story.

56
00:03:12,417 --> 00:03:15,897
The title was readable. The size was right. The

57
00:03:15,897 --> 00:03:19,378
shape was clear. Her stomach still tightened. The checks

58
00:03:19,378 --> 00:03:22,858
had settled practical questions, not provided immunity from being

59
00:03:22,858 --> 00:03:26,339
judged.

60
00:03:26,759 --> 00:03:30,767
She chose one final working session. Enough time to

61
00:03:30,767 --> 00:03:34,775
repair the broken letter and clean a distracting mark.

62
00:03:34,775 --> 00:03:38,783
Not enough time to begin the entire sky again.

63
00:03:39,203 --> 00:03:42,450
When the new color tempted her, she covered its

64
00:03:42,450 --> 00:03:45,697
jar. When another idea arrived, she wrote it on

65
00:03:45,697 --> 00:03:48,944
a separate sheet. A future possibility did not have

66
00:03:48,944 --> 00:03:52,191
to become tonight's emergency.

67
00:03:52,611 --> 00:03:55,931
The repair went badly once. A red line spread

68
00:03:55,931 --> 00:03:59,251
beyond its intended edge. She felt the familiar surge:

69
00:03:59,251 --> 00:04:02,572
start over. Instead, she blotted the ink and inspected

70
00:04:02,572 --> 00:04:05,892
the damage.

71
00:04:06,312 --> 00:04:09,330
A small border could absorb the mark. She tried

72
00:04:09,330 --> 00:04:12,348
it on spare paper, then on the print. The

73
00:04:12,348 --> 00:04:15,367
result differed from the image in her head. It

74
00:04:15,367 --> 00:04:18,385
still worked.

75
00:04:18,805 --> 00:04:22,223
This time, her process had an exit: define the

76
00:04:22,223 --> 00:04:25,640
piece, make it, check the agreed needs, release it.

77
00:04:25,640 --> 00:04:29,058
New ideas could return in the next piece, instead

78
00:04:29,058 --> 00:04:32,475
of trapping this one.

79
00:04:32,895 --> 00:04:36,262
She cleaned the tools and wrapped the block. Each

80
00:04:36,262 --> 00:04:39,628
object leaving the table made her uneasy. Tidying was

81
00:04:39,628 --> 00:04:42,995
no longer preparation for more work. It was evidence

82
00:04:42,995 --> 00:04:46,362
that work could end.

83
00:04:46,782 --> 00:04:50,781
Then she sent her friend a photograph and the

84
00:04:50,781 --> 00:04:54,781
frame dimensions. Before she could add three paragraphs of

85
00:04:54,781 --> 00:04:58,780
apology, a reply arrived: great, see you tomorrow.

86
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:02,311
She slept badly anyway. In the dark, she redesigned

87
00:05:02,311 --> 00:05:05,421
the sky twice. The urge to revise did not

88
00:05:05,421 --> 00:05:08,532
respect the fact that the ink was dry and

89
00:05:08,532 --> 00:05:11,642
the poster was packed.

90
00:05:12,062 --> 00:05:16,012
On Saturday, she walked to the exhibition with both

91
00:05:16,012 --> 00:05:19,961
hands around the frame. The package felt heavier whenever

92
00:05:19,961 --> 00:05:23,910
she imagined turning back. She kept walking without pretending

93
00:05:23,910 --> 00:05:27,860
to feel brave.

94
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:31,043
The room was smaller than the one in her

95
00:05:31,043 --> 00:05:33,805
head. A table rocked on an uneven floor. Someone

96
00:05:33,805 --> 00:05:36,568
was searching for tape. Her corner had a slightly

97
00:05:36,568 --> 00:05:39,331
crooked hook.

98
00:05:39,751 --> 00:05:43,059
June hung the print. From that distance, she immediately

99
00:05:43,059 --> 00:05:46,367
saw a flaw in the border. Her hand lifted

100
00:05:46,367 --> 00:05:49,675
toward it, although there was nothing she could fix

101
00:05:49,675 --> 00:05:52,983
without taking everything down.

102
00:05:53,403 --> 00:05:56,395
A visitor paused and asked about the small figure

103
00:05:56,395 --> 00:05:59,387
beneath the moon. June explained the scene. The visitor

104
00:05:59,387 --> 00:06:02,380
told her what it reminded him of. He had

105
00:06:02,380 --> 00:06:05,372
not mentioned the border.

106
00:06:05,792 --> 00:06:09,318
Another person passed without stopping. That hurt more than

107
00:06:09,318 --> 00:06:12,844
she expected. Then she watched someone pass a painting

108
00:06:12,844 --> 00:06:16,371
she admired. Attention was uneven; it was not a

109
00:06:16,371 --> 00:06:19,897
final measurement of worth.

110
00:06:20,317 --> 00:06:23,622
She wrote down two useful observations after the exhibition.

111
00:06:23,622 --> 00:06:26,928
Make the lettering stronger next time. Leave room for

112
00:06:26,928 --> 00:06:30,234
a lighter shadow. Improvement finally had somewhere to go

113
00:06:30,234 --> 00:06:33,539
that was not backward.

114
00:06:33,959 --> 00:06:36,829
On Sunday, she put a fresh sheet on the

115
00:06:36,829 --> 00:06:39,699
table. The finished poster leaned against the wall, imperfect

116
00:06:39,699 --> 00:06:42,568
and complete. Beside it, another piece was allowed to

117
00:06:42,568 --> 00:06:45,438
begin.

118
00:06:45,858 --> 00:06:48,891
Care about the work. Give it a clear brief.

119
00:06:48,891 --> 00:06:51,924
Correct what matters. But do not make one unfinished

120
00:06:51,924 --> 00:06:54,957
piece responsible for proving that you deserve to make

121
00:06:54,957 --> 00:06:57,989
the next one.
